        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Expedition Six crew began its four-month stay aboard the International Space Station (ISS) when Endeavour docked to the orbiting laboratory on the Shuttle's third flight day. Expedition Six crewmembers, Commander Ken Bowersox and Flight Engineers Donald Pettit and  Nikolai Budarin, were launched aboard STS-108 on December 5, 2001. During their stay, the crew's focus was on science, crew health (highlighted by approximately two hours of physical exercise for each crewmember each day), and station operations.
<P>
No human space flights were scheduled to visit the Expedition Six crew during their stay on the space station. The only spacecraft scheduled to dock with the station before the Expedition Six crew returned home was an unpiloted Progress supply vehicle. Progress 10 brought equipment, supplies, and fuel to the space station. 
<P>
Bowersox and Budarin performed a spacewalk in which Budarin became the first Russian to perform a spacewalk in a U.S. space suit from the Joint Airlock. Pettit provided intravehicular support, quarterbacking the spacewalk from inside the station. He also operated Canadarm2, the station's robotic arm. 
<P>
The spacewalk was scheduled to last approximately six hours. Bowersox and Budarin each released nine Radiator Beam Launch Locks to secure the accordion-like radiators in the folded position. Next, Bowersox reconfigured the power harness of the Squib Firing Unit used to release the radiator panels for deployment. Then, with help from Budarin, he deployed a UHF antenna on the P1 integrated truss structure, providing redundancy for a similar antenna. 
<P>
The crew also worked on several scientific investigations, continuing some experiments begun during earlier Expeditions and initiating new experiments. Many of the experiments looked at effects of long-term space flight, using crewmembers as subjects. Others used the microgravity of low Earth orbit for basic scientific studies, as well as investigations of processes that could be used in manufacturing with direct benefits to people on Earth. 
<P>
Among the new investigations performed by Expedition Six were two series of experiments to be conducted in the Microgravity Science Glovebox (MSG). The MSG was brought to the station with the Expedition Five crew last June. Expedition Five crewmembers successfully completed one series of MSG experiments and began another, which continued during Expedition Six. The new MSG experiments were Coarsening in Solid Liquid Mixture (CSLM) and InSPACE. Protein Crystal Growth Single-locker Thermal Enclosure System (PCGSTES), launched with the Expedition Six crew on STS-113, also flew on Expeditions Two, Four and Five. The PCGSTES provides a temperature-controlled environment for growing high-quality protein crystals (different from those on earlier missions) in microgravity for later analyses on the ground to determine the proteins' molecular structure. This research could have applications in medicine and agriculture. 
<P>
Life sciences experiments and payloads that began during Expedition Six were: 
<P>
<UL>
<LI><B>Chromosomal Aberrations in Blood Lymphocytes of Astronauts (Chromosome)</B> will study space radiation on humans. The expected results will provide a better knowledge of the genetic risk of astronauts in space and can help to optimize radiation shielding.</LI>
<LI><B>Foot/Ground Reaction Forces During Space Flight</B> seeks to characterize the load on the lower body and muscle activity in crewmembers while working in the microgravity environment of the space station.</LI>
</UL>
<P>
Life sciences experiments continuing from earlier Expeditions were: 
<P>
<UL>
<LI><B>Renal Stone</B>, research into a possible preventive medication for kidney stone formation.</LI>
<LI><B>Pulmonary Function in Flight (PuFF)</B>, an experiment examining long-term lung function in microgravity.</LI>
<LI><B>Extra Vehicular Activity Radiation Monitoring (EVARM)</B>, sets of three sensors worn in pockets in U.S. EVA suits that will help determine the levels of radiation received to the skin, eyes, and blood-forming organs of crewmembers, and ways to mitigate exposure.</LI>
<LI><B>Promoting Sensorimotor Response to Generalizability (Mobility)</B>, a pre- and postflight investigation designed to study the changes in posture and gait after long-duration space flight.</LI>
<LI><B>Effect of Prolonged Spaceflight on Human Skeletal Muscle (Biopsy)</B>, pre- and postflight tests on crewmembers that will help to determine the progression and extent of functional and structural change in limb skeletal muscle during prolonged space flight.</LI>
<LI><B>Spaceflight-induced Reactivation of Latent Epstein-Barr Virus (Epstein-Barr)</B>, a pre- and postfight investigation designed to study changes in human immune function.</LI>
<LI><B>Subregional Bone</B>, a pre- and postflight experiment designed to study changes in bone density caused by long-duration space flight.</LI>
</UL>
<P>
The Expedition Six crewmembers returned to Earth on May 3, 2003, aboard the Soyuz TMA-1 spacecraft.
